* EBS snapshot : 
>> EBS snapshot are point in time images/copies of your EBS volumes. 
>> At the point of snapshot , only processed data is came in snapshot , running or ongoing processes are not inclueded in snapshot. 
>> Snapshot data is stored in S3 storage managed by Amazon. We can't access them directly. You can only access them through EC2 API but location of data are not known. 
>> Per AWS account , upto 5000 EBS volumes can be created. 
>> Per AWS account upto 10000 snapshot can be created.
>> While EBS volumes are AZ specific ,snapshot are region specific.
>> Any AZ in region can use snapshot to create EBS Volume. 
>> To migrate to EBS from one AZ to another , create a snapshot and create EBS volume from snapshot in intended AZ. 
>> You can create a snapshot to an EBs volume of the same or large size than orginal volume size from which snapshot was initally created. 
>> You can take snapshot of non root EBS volume  in running EC2 instance, means EBS volume can access while snapshot process is going on. 
>> Snapshot created immediately  but it will stay in pending state.First time snapshot take more time as compared to incremental snapshot. 
>> To take complete snapshot to your non root EBS volume it is recommended stop or unmount the volume.
Note : to take snapshot of root volume , it should be in stopped/detach can't take snapshot in running state. 

* Incremental snapshot 
>> EBS snapshot stored incrementallly . 
>> Incremental snapshot will capture only updated data. 
>> If previous snapshot is deleted then non updated data will move to next snapshot before deleting snapshot , to secure data. 
>> First snapshot will be exact copy of EBS volume, later snapshot will be incremental. 


