 *EBS Enryption key 
>> To encrytpt a volume  or a snapshot ,you need a encryption key, called customer master key ( CMK )  and are managed by AWS key mangament services ( KMS ) 
>> when encrypting the first EBS volume , AWS KMS create default CMK . can't shared with anyone . 
>> This key is used for your first volume encryption, encrytpin of snapshots  created from this volume and sequennt volume created from snapshot. 
>> After that each newly encrypted volume is encrypted with unique AES-256 encryption key. 

* Changing Encryption key 
>>  You can't encrytion key to encrypt on existing encryption snapshot or volume. 
>> if you want to change the key , create a copy of the snapshot and specific during the copy procced that you want to re- encrypt the copy with different key. 
>> This is came handy when you snapshot is encrypted with default key, default cmk key can't be shared outside your account. 
>> By default , only account owner can create volume from the account snapshot
>> You can share your un-encrypted snapshot with AWS community by making them public, but this is not best practice.rather share snapshot with selected account. 
>> You can't make encrypted snapshot public. 
>> You can share your encrypted snapshot  with specific AWS account as follows
  -- make sure that you use non default CMK
  -- configure cross account permission in order to grant access to account which you wan to share.if Cross account is disable then other can't access snapshot even with key shared. 
  -- Without this ,other account will not be able to copy the snapshot , nor will able to create volumw of snapshot. 
>> You can only make copy of the snapshot when it is fully saved at S3 storage. 
>> Amazon S3 server side encryption ( SSE ) protect the snapshot data during copying.
>> You can have upto 5 snapshot copy request running in single destination per account. 
  
Note : Summary - You can't use default CMK for sharing , need to take snapshot and re encrypt with new key and then share. 
* For AWS account with whom snapshot is shared. 
 >> They must first create own copy of snapshot.
 >> Then create the copy of encrypted snapshot with new key, so that he can access the snapshot even if cross account permissions are taken by the owner of snapshot.  
 
 
 
 
 
 
 

 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 


























































































































































































































































































































































































































